<h2>Lighting Around Water Is a Different Discipline</h2>
<p>A pool at night is a 400-square-foot mirror. Every fixture you can see from the patio is reflected in the water, which means a single badly placed spotlight shows up twice. At the same time, the pool deck is the most important place on the property for safety lighting: wet limestone, coping edges, tanning ledges and steps all need to be readable without being bright. Pool landscape lighting Austin homeowners are happiest with manages both problems by lighting the things around the pool, not the pool itself, and by keeping sources below eye level and shielded.</p>
<p>There is also a safety and code dimension. Electrical rules for anything near a pool are strict, including minimum distances for low-voltage fixtures, transformer placement and bonding requirements. Austin Landscape Lighting installs the landscape side of pool lighting in coordination with your pool builder or pool electrician, who handles the in-pool and in-spa lights. That division keeps the project compliant and keeps each trade doing what it does best.</p>

<h2>Our Design Method Around Pools</h2>
<h3>Start with what reflects</h3>
<p>We walk the deck at dusk and look at the water from every seating position, including inside the house. Anything that would reflect as a bright point is either moved, shielded or aimed away. The best pool lighting typically uses no fixtures at all within the first several feet of the water's edge, relying instead on light from beyond it.</p>
<h3>Light the perimeter, borrow for the deck</h3>
<p>Uplit trees and shrubs beyond the pool, grazed limestone walls and lit outdoor kitchens create a glowing perimeter. That perimeter light bounces across the deck surface at a low level, enough to see the coping and the steps. Where more is needed, we use hardscape lights set into retaining walls, raised beam walls and planter caps, aimed down across the deck. Recessed step lights handle any grade change.</p>
<h3>Treat water features as sculpture</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Waterfalls and sheer descents.</strong> A narrow spot aimed across the falling water from the side catches the texture of the stream without lighting the catch basin. Light from directly in front flattens it.</li>
<li><strong>Bubblers and deck jets.</strong> Small submersible 12V fixtures rated for the application light the arc of water from below; the pool builder typically installs these and we coordinate aiming.</li>
<li><strong>Fountains and spillover spas.</strong> A grazing fixture on the stone face of a raised spa shows the water sheeting over it. Keep the output low; wet stone reflects more than you expect.</li>
<li><strong>Ponds and natural features.</strong> Submersible fixtures beneath a waterfall and uplights on the boulders behind it create depth. We avoid lighting the pond surface, which only shows debris.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Technical notes</h3>
<p>Fixtures are brass and copper, which resist chlorinated and salt-system splash far better than aluminum. Color temperature is 2700K throughout to keep the stone warm and the water inviting; the cool blue glow belongs inside the pool, if anywhere, not on the deck. Transformers are mounted well away from the water at a GFCI-protected outlet, and cable runs are kept out of the pool equipment trench. Where fixtures sit near the water within the distances that electrical rules address, we follow those requirements and coordinate bonding with the pool electrician. If you are planning a new pool, involving us before the deck is poured lets us sleeve conduit under the coping, which eliminates visible cable forever. The <a href="/tools/lighting-visualizer/">lighting visualizer</a> is a useful way to preview the effect on your own backyard photo.</p>

<h2>Austin-Specific Considerations</h2>
<p><strong>Limestone and flagstone decks.</strong> The light-colored stone used around most Austin pools reflects a great deal of light, which is good for safety and means fixture output can stay low. We frequently use 1 to 3 watt hardscape fixtures where a client expected something much brighter.</p>
<p><strong>Heat and chemistry.</strong> Pool decks are among the hottest surfaces on the property in July and are regularly splashed with treated water. Fixture bodies and lenses are chosen for both, and we avoid plastic lenses that cloud within two seasons.</p>
<p><strong>Trees overhead.</strong> Live oaks and cedar elms shading a pool are perfect moonlighting candidates. Downlights mounted in the canopy produce soft dappled light across the deck and surface with no fixtures at ground level at all, and nothing reflects because the source is far above the sightline. See <a href="/services/garden-and-tree-lighting/">garden and tree lighting</a> for how we mount in trees.</p>
<p><strong>Dark-sky and neighbors.</strong> Backyards in Westlake Hills, Lakeway and Dripping Springs often sit above or beside neighbors. Shielded fixtures aimed downward and inward, 2700K lamps and a timer that shuts the perimeter off late at night keep the installation neighborly and consistent with Hill Country dark-sky awareness.</p>

